@page "/formulas"

<PageTitle>Formulas · Caustikon</PageTitle>

<h1>Formulas</h1>
<p class="lede">Every equation the packages evaluate, with the conventions that make its numbers comparable to a catalog, and the type that implements it.</p>

<h2>Refraction</h2>
<div class="equation">n<sub>i</sub> sin θ<sub>i</sub> = n<sub>t</sub> sin θ<sub>t</sub></div>
<p>
    <code>Dielectric.RefractUnit</code> takes unit vectors rather than angles. With η = n<sub>i</sub>/n<sub>t</sub>, the incident
    direction I and a normal N pointing into the incident medium, the transmitted direction is
    η·I<sub>tangent</sub> − √(1 − η²|I<sub>tangent</sub>|²)·N. When the square root's argument is negative the call reports total
    internal reflection; within eight units of single-precision epsilon of zero it reports the critical angle and returns the
    tangent direction. Both indices must share a reference medium: catalog indices are relative to air, so air is 1.
</p>

<h2>Fresnel power reflectance</h2>
<div class="equation">
    R<sub>s</sub> = ((n<sub>i</sub> cos θ<sub>i</sub> − n<sub>t</sub> cos θ<sub>t</sub>) / (n<sub>i</sub> cos θ<sub>i</sub> + n<sub>t</sub> cos θ<sub>t</sub>))²<br />
    R<sub>p</sub> = ((n<sub>t</sub> cos θ<sub>i</sub> − n<sub>i</sub> cos θ<sub>t</sub>) / (n<sub>t</sub> cos θ<sub>i</sub> + n<sub>i</sub> cos θ<sub>t</sub>))²
</div>
<p>
    <code>Dielectric.Fresnel</code> returns both polarizations; unpolarized light is their mean. Beyond the critical angle both are 1.
    Two surfaces in series must be composed per polarization, (T<sub>s1</sub>T<sub>s2</sub> + T<sub>p1</sub>T<sub>p2</sub>)/2, because
    light is partially polarized after the first; the playground does this. At normal incidence both reduce to
    R₀ = ((n<sub>i</sub> − n<sub>t</sub>)/(n<sub>i</sub> + n<sub>t</sub>))², <code>Dielectric.NormalReflectance</code>, and Schlick's
    approximation R₀ + (1 − R₀)(1 − cos θ)⁵ is <code>Dielectric.Schlick</code>; it does not know about total internal reflection.
</p>

<h2>Dispersion</h2>
<p>λ is in micrometers in every form. Each model carries the wavelength interval its coefficients were fitted over and refuses to extrapolate.</p>
<h3>Sellmeier — <code>Sellmeier</code>, <code>Sellmeier3</code></h3>
<div class="equation">n² − 1 = offset + Σ<sub>i</sub> B<sub>i</sub> λ² / (λ² − C<sub>i</sub>)</div>
<p>
    C<sub>i</sub> is the squared resonance wavelength in µm². SCHOTT, OHARA and CDGM publish this form. A source that lists the
    resonance wavelengths themselves is imported by squaring them. A positive C<sub>i</sub> puts a pole at 1000·√C<sub>i</sub> nm;
    a model whose interval contains an active pole cannot be constructed. Negative C<sub>i</sub>, which several CDGM fits carry,
    has no pole and is accepted.
</p>
<h3>Polynomial — <code>Polynomial</code></h3>
<div class="equation">n² = a₀ + Σ<sub>i</sub> a<sub>i</sub> λ<sup>p<sub>i</sub></sup></div>
<p>
    The older "SCHOTT formula", still used by HOYA, SUMITA, HIKARI and part of OHARA, with powers 2, −2, −4, −6, −8 and sometimes −10, −12.
    Even integer powers are evaluated by repeated multiplication or division applied to the coefficient, so a small coefficient over a
    short wavelength cannot overflow before the ratio is formed.
</p>
<h3>Cauchy — <code>Cauchy</code>, <code>Cauchy3</code></h3>
<div class="equation">n = a₀ + Σ<sub>i</sub> a<sub>i</sub> λ<sup>p<sub>i</sub></sup></div>
<p>The same series in n rather than n²; index-matching liquids are catalogued this way.</p>

<h2>Abbe number</h2>
<div class="equation">ν<sub>d</sub> = (n<sub>d</sub> − 1) / (n<sub>F</sub> − n<sub>C</sub>)</div>
<p>
    d = 587.5618 nm (He), F = 486.1327 nm (H), C = 656.2725 nm (H): <code>FraunhoferLines</code>. ν<sub>d</sub> divides by a difference of two
    nearly equal numbers, which is why the catalog tests use it: an error in the fourth decimal of a coefficient shows up there
    and nowhere else.
</p>

<h2>Absorption</h2>
<div class="equation">α = 4πk / λ &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; τ<sub>i</sub>(d) = exp(−α d)</div>
<p>
    <code>TabulatedExtinction</code> holds the manufacturer's k(λ) and interpolates it linearly in log k, because k spans several
    orders of magnitude across the visible. τ<sub>i</sub> is internal transmittance: bulk only, no surface reflection. Manufacturers
    print τ<sub>i</sub> for 10 mm and 25 mm; the catalog reproduces SCHOTT's N-BK7 columns within their printed precision.
</p>

<h2>Temperature</h2>
<div class="equation">Δn<sub>abs</sub>(λ, T) = (n² − 1)/(2n) · [D₀ΔT + D₁ΔT² + D₂ΔT³ + (E₀ΔT + E₁ΔT²)/(λ² − λ<sub>TK</sub>²)]</div>
<p>
    <code>ThermalDispersion</code>, the form from SCHOTT TIE-19, with ΔT from 20 °C. It gives the change of the absolute index. The
    relative index also depends on how air's index moves with temperature, which is not modelled. For N-BK7 the result matches the
    datasheet's Δn<sub>abs</sub>/ΔT in all nine printed cells.
</p>

<h2>Colour</h2>
<div class="equation">
    X = Σ S(λ) T(λ) x̄(λ) / Σ S(λ) ȳ(λ), &nbsp; likewise Y, Z<br />
    [R G B]<sub>linear</sub> = M<sub>sRGB</sub> · [X Y Z], then the sRGB transfer curve
</div>
<p>
    <code>GlassColour</code> integrates over 360–830 nm at 1 nm with the CIE 1931 2° observer and illuminant D65, normalized so that a
    perfect transmitter has Y = 1. Y is therefore the luminous transmittance. Channels are clamped to [0, 1] and the result says
    whether clamping happened.
</p>
